  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTuesday morning, ensembles show three 500-mb features that \ndrive weather across the forecast area: a high across the \nSoutheastern United States, a trough across the Western United \nStates, and broad troughing across portions of Southeast Canada.\nA shortwave lifting across the Midwest would be associated with\nnortheasterly surface flow across the forecast area, setting \nthe stage for cooler conditions through the duration of the long\nterm period. Surface highs Tuesday are currently forecast in \nthe mid to upper 70s, but lower percentiles of the NBM show that\nhighs as low as the mid to upper 60s can't be ruled out. \nTemperatures are favored to remain lower than recent weeks \nWednesday and Thursday even though southerly flow would \ngenerally be favored between the high across the southeast and \ntrough across the Western United States. This would be caused by\ncontinued easterly to northeasterly surface flow from a strong \nsurface high to the northeast of the forecast region, and \npotential cloud cover and precipitation from convergence across \nKansas. Currently, surface highs are forecast in the mid 70s to \nlower 80s Wednesday and Thursday. Temperatures could reach a \nlittle higher than the current forecast depending on the \nnortherly extent of the southerly flow, and whether any solar \nheating will be allowed to develop. \n\nAs mentioned before, precipitation is favored to occur Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. Tuesday, all areas in the forecast area have \nat least a 25% chance or greater of receiving more than 0.1 \ninches of rain according to NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance, \nwith areas along and east of a line from Oberlin, KS to Tribune,\nKS showing a 50-75% chance. Most of this precipitation is \nexpected to be along the cold front in the area, and is not \nfavored to be convective in nature, as LREF guidance shows an \n80% chance or higher across the forecast region that surface \nCAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) will remain below \n1000 J/kg. Convergence across Kansas from southerly flow meeting\neasterly flow would continue to support precipitation Wednesday\nand Thursday. NBM 24 hr guidance shows Wednesday to be the \nbetter day for precipitation, with a 50-75% chance across nearly\nthe entire forecast region for greater than 0.1 inches of rain.\nAlso, there may be a better chance for convective activity \nWednesday, as LREF guidance shows a 40-45% chance of surface \nCAPE greater than 1000 J/kg between Kansas Highways 25 and 27. \nGFS and EC soundings show sfc-6km shear between 20 and 35 kts, \nwhich would suggest that most thunderstorms would be sub-severe,\nwith perhaps a storm or two that could produce winds up to 60 \nmph. Confidence in severe storms occurring Wednesday is around \n5% or less. Precipitation is not favored to be convective \nThursday, as instability looks to decrease again. \n\nThe trough across the Western United States looks to be slowly \nmoving toward the forecast area during the duration of the long \nterm, though it is not entirely certain when the trough will \nmove in. Ensemble guidance mutes this feature by Friday morning.\nAdditionally, GFS and EC deterministics continue to show this \nwave as a weaker one, which would indicate most of the \nuncertainty is in association with the timing of the trough. As \nlong as the trough remains west of the forecast area, conditions\nsimilar to Tuesday through Thursday would be more likely. \nHowever, conditions may cool a little more once the wave moves \nover the region and northerly flow reestablishes itself. NBM \n25th percentile solutions and lower show highs in the mid 70s or\nlower Friday and Saturday in association with this trough \nmoving overhead.",
